    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2074fdd5-5e10-543a-9551-133b599a3308",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186083243/outputs-1/curve-262315be0f881f43c6105311.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t^{3} + 2 t^{2},\\,y=2 t^{2},\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3 t^{2} + 4 t,\\,y'(t)=4 t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(4 t\\right)}{\\left(3 t^{2} + 4 t\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3 t + 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=3,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t=3} & =\\frac{4}{3 \\left(3\\right) + 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "5031bf22-727e-5f31-bd7d-718fb4cbee64",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
