      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=5 t^{3} + 1,\\,y=4 t^{4} + 3 t^{2},\\,0\\le t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=3.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=15 t^{2},\\,y'(t)=2 t \\left(8 t^{2} + 3\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(2 t \\left(8 t^{2} + 3\\right)\\right)}{\\left(15 t^{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\left(8 t^{2} + 3\\right)}{15 t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=3,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2 \\left(8 \\left(3\\right)^{2} + 3\\right)}{15 \\left(3\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{150}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
